    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "dc6bd083_686c26e4",
        "filename": "drivers/block/zram/zram_drv.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 1215,
      "author": {
        "id": 3489357
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-03-04T01:30:11Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Each write request requires additional physical page for payload. So what we have is [a sketch, not a real code] something like:\n\n```\nfor_each_slot() {\n   bio \u003d alloc_bio()\n   bio-\u003epage \u003d alloc_page()\n   zs_malloc_read(bio-\u003epage)  // or decompress to bio-\u003epage\n   submit_bio(bio)\n}\n```\n\nWe have an unbound number of slots to writeback and relying on block layer throttling is too risky - H/W `nr_requests` can be many thousands, which can put significant memory pressure on the system. One thing to note here is that writeback can be used in all sorts of environments (e.g. servers with per-CPU blk-mq), not only as swap writeback on embedded devices.  So we have a fixed size pool of preallocated requests (max batch-sizxe) which we use to control writeback pressure.",
      "parentUuid": "f3100d71_3cf1f336",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1188,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 1215,
        "endChar": 0
      },
      "revId": "41f9b66000690aa7bd9f333b7e751a8fb36e497c",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b45ee4f3_01f3b31c",
        "filename": "drivers/block/zram/zram_drv.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 1215,
      "author": {
        "id": 1275554
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-03-04T23:51:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It\u0027s also applicable for other subsystem. For example, mmap(4GB) and keep touching all the pages, which will create a lot memory pressure, too. Other example, dmabuf alloc from the user process with 4GB allocation. I believe we can find a lot places to be vulnerable for that but we don\u0027t put such throttling logic for every subsystem because finally, VM will throttle transparently once memory pressure is severe. Do we have reason why zram is something special here?",
      "parentUuid": "dc6bd083_686c26e4",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1188,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 1215,
        "endChar": 0
      },
      "revId": "41f9b66000690aa7bd9f333b7e751a8fb36e497c",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4d5d4460_e55b34de",
        "filename": "drivers/block/zram/zram_drv.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 1215,
      "author": {
        "id": 1060234
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-03-05T03:08:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "For writeback, the user has no reason to expect that the operation requires a lot of memory. If the user touches a bunch of memory or allocates a DMA buf, of course that\u0027s going to allocate a lot of memory. But as a user, I would be very surprised if performing zram writeback inexplicably allocated 4GB, or even 128MiB of memory.\n\nPrefetch is a bit different, since we are explicitly pulling data from disk into memory. That said, prefetch occurs during app launch, which is often memory constrained and performance sensitive. So it\u0027s definitely preferable to avoid allocating more memory than we strictly need. With compressed writeback, arbitrarily queuing io could result in a memory footprint 2-3x larger than is actually necessary (depending on how much of the prefetched data the app actually faults out of zram). That could definitely cause app launch to go into direct reclaim and jank.",
      "parentUuid": "b45ee4f3_01f3b31c",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1188,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 1215,
        "endChar": 0
      },
      "revId": "41f9b66000690aa7bd9f333b7e751a8fb36e497c",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2a14e316_be411dc1",
        "filename": "drivers/block/zram/zram_drv.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 1215,
      "author": {
        "id": 3489357
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-03-05T06:57:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I agree with David\u0027s comment.\n\n\u003e Do we have reason why zram is something special here?\n\nIs that a fair comparison? The first one (mem alloc) is a deliberate request to allocate X resources, with clear expectations. The second one (writeback/prefetch) is an OP request with unpredictable and unconstrained resources usage.\n\nI like how in-flight sets clear upper boundary and gives us a lot of control. It is also less failure prone, as we pre-allocate a fixed number of requests (with physical page for payload per request) and then just use them. Doing \"alloc_page/kmalloc/bio_alloc\" in a loop adds more points of failure.\n\nWe already have pool of requests and num-in-flight knob in upstream zram for writeback (we upstreamed it in Nov of last year), so let\u0027s just use it for prefetch too?",
      "parentUuid": "4d5d4460_e55b34de",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1188,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 1215,
        "endChar": 0
      },
      "revId": "41f9b66000690aa7bd9f333b7e751a8fb36e497c",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "276f5e9b_0e925a01",
        "filename": "drivers/block/zram/zram_drv.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 1215,
      "author": {
        "id": 1275554
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-03-05T08:03:10Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "```\nFor writeback, the user has no reason to expect that the operation requires a lot of memory. If the user touches a bunch of memory or allocates a DMA buf, of course that\u0027s going to allocate a lot of memory. But as a user, I would be very surprised if performing zram writeback inexplicably allocated 4GB, or even 128MiB of memory.\n```\n\nFirst, users shouldn\u0027t be surprised because they can see exactly what they are requesting. For instance, if they specify page_indexes\u003d200-300, the scope is clear. If they target \"incompressible\" or \"huge\" pages, they can check mm_stat to see the counts. If they target \"idle\" pages, they can check the status via block_state. In all these cases, the resource requirements are transparent to the user.\n\nSecond, this is a matter of implementation. If we implement the loop like this:\n```\nfor (;;) {\n    page \u003d alloc_pages\n    submit_bio(page); \n}\n```\nThen submit_bio will throttle automatically once all in-flight requests are consumed. If a user believes the backing device allows too much data in-flight (e.g., 128MB), they should tune their device\u0027s queue depth accordingly. The issue with the current writeback implementation is that it attempts to allocate a large array of pages before submission:\n```\nfor (; ;) {\n   pages_array \u003d alloc_pages()\n}\n\nfor (page from page_array) {\n   submit_bio(page);\n}\n```\nThis bypasses existing throttling logic. Instead of using the block layer\u0027s native mechanisms, zram is essentially reinventing the wheel.\n\n```\nPrefetch is a bit different, since we are explicitly pulling data from disk into memory. That said, prefetch occurs during app launch, which is often memory constrained and performance sensitive. So it\u0027s definitely preferable to avoid allocating more memory than we strictly need. With compressed writeback, \narbitrarily queuing io could result in a memory footprint 2-3x larger than is actually necessary (depending on how much of the prefetched data the app actually faults out of zram). That could definitely cause app launch to go into direct reclaim and jank.\n```\n\nAgain, it\u0027s not \"arbitrarily\" queuing I/O. Unlike writeback, the current prefetch implementation performs alloc_page followed immediately by submit_bio. This ensures that the process is strictly governed by the backing device\u0027s queue depth. If the resulting pressure is too high for a specific system, the user can and should adjust their device\u0027s queue settings.\n\n```\nWe already have pool of requests and num-in-flight knob in upstream zram for writeback (we upstreamed it in Nov of last year), so let\u0027s just use it for prefetch too?\n```\n\nI am not opposed to using the existing logic for the sake of consistency. However, my concern is our tendency to make things more complicated than necessary from the outset.\n\nAccumulating complex logic without evidence of real-world issues often leads to a codebase that is difficult to maintain. While I acknowledge that unforeseen issues can occur and that some level of throttling in zram may be justified, I prefer to address these based on real use cases. This allows us to understand the problem better, discuss the best solution, and maintain a clear record in the git history to avoid repeating past mistakes, rather than adding complexity for hypothetical problems.",
      "parentUuid": "4d5d4460_e55b34de",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1188,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 1215,
        "endChar": 0
      },
      "revId": "41f9b66000690aa7bd9f333b7e751a8fb36e497c",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
