      "writtenOn": "2026-03-05T17:47:37Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e First, users shouldn\u0027t be surprised because they can see exactly what they are requesting. For instance, if they specify page_indexes\u003d200-300, the scope is clear. If they target \"incompressible\" or \"huge\" pages, they can check mm_stat to see the counts. If they target \"idle\" pages, they can check the status via block_state. In all these cases, the resource requirements are transparent to the user.\n\nI don\u0027t think that\u0027s a realistic interpretation. That\u0027s analogous to saying \"it\u0027s okay if scp\u0027ing a 100GiB file requires 100GiB of memory\".\n\n\u003e Then submit_bio will throttle automatically once all in-flight requests are consumed.\n\nI didn\u0027t realize this was the case, either. If the block layer has reasonable throttling, there is significantly less benefit to having additional throttling in zram. I agree that it\u0027s probably not worth the additional complexity, at least without a report of a concrete problem.",
